-- Q1 [Electrolysis & Faraday's Law · easy · Skill 1 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans A] (g13)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Electrolysis & Faraday''s Law',
    $qt13$During the electrolytic refining of aluminum, solid aluminum metal is deposited at the cathode from molten aluminum salt by the reduction half-reaction \(\text{Al}^{3+} + 3e^- \rightarrow \text{Al}(s)\). Based on this half-reaction, how many moles of electrons must pass through the cell to deposit exactly one mole of aluminum metal?$qt13$,
    $op13$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(3\\) moles of electrons"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(1\\) mole of electrons"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(2\\) moles of electrons"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(6\\) moles of electrons"}]$op13$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex13$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is A because the cathode half-reaction \(\text{Al}^{3+} + 3e^- \rightarrow \text{Al}(s)\) shows that each \(\text{Al}^{3+}\) ion must gain \(3\) electrons to be reduced to one neutral aluminum atom. Scaling this up by Avogadro's number, depositing one mole of aluminum atoms requires one mole of \(\text{Al}^{3+}\) ions to each gain \(3\) electrons, so \(3\) moles of electrons must pass through the cell. In general, the number of moles of electrons needed to deposit one mole of a metal equals the charge \(n\) on its ion, and here \(n = 3\). (Choice B) \(1\) mole of electrons would deposit one mole of a singly charged metal ion such as \(\text{Ag}^+\) (\(\text{Ag}^+ + e^- \rightarrow \text{Ag}\)), but \(\text{Al}^{3+}\) carries a \(3+\) charge and requires three electrons per ion. (Choice C) \(2\) moles of electrons would deposit one mole of a doubly charged metal ion such as \(\text{Cu}^{2+}\) or \(\text{Ni}^{2+}\); this uses the wrong ionic charge for aluminum. (Choice D) \(6\) moles of electrons is double the required amount, which would deposit two moles of aluminum, not one; it likely comes from incorrectly adding or combining the charge with another factor rather than reading \(n = 3\) directly from the half-reaction. Skill 1$ex13$,
    'easy', '4C', $cc13$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc13$, 'general-chemistry', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'B', 'misconception', $a13B$Assumes every metal ion gains a single electron (treats n = 1 universally) regardless of the ion's actual charge.$a13B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'adjacent_fact', $a13C$Applies the correct n-electrons rule but with the charge of a common 2+ ion (Cu2+/Ni2+) instead of the 3+ charge of aluminum.$a13C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'scale_unit_error', $a13D$Doubles the correct electron count (deposits two moles instead of one), confusing the per-mole stoichiometry by an extra factor of two.$a13D$ FROM q;

-- Q2 [Electrolysis & Faraday's Law · medium · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans A] (g17)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Electrolysis & Faraday''s Law',
    $qt17$A constant current of \(9.65\ \text{A}\) is passed for \(50.0\ \text{min}\) through molten sodium chloride in an electrolytic cell, and chlorine gas is liberated at the anode according to \(2\text{Cl}^- \rightarrow \text{Cl}_2(g) + 2e^-\). Assuming \(100\%\) current efficiency, taking the Faraday constant as \(F = 96{,}485\ \text{C/mol e}^-\), and collecting the gas at STP where the molar volume is \(22.4\ \text{L/mol}\), what volume of \(\text{Cl}_2\) gas is evolved at the anode?$qt17$,
    $op17$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(3.36\\ \\text{L}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(6.72\\ \\text{L}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(1.68\\ \\text{L}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(0.0560\\ \\text{L}\\)"}]$op17$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex17$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is A because the volume of evolved \(\text{Cl}_2\) is found by tracking charge through to moles of gas. First convert the time to seconds: \(t = 50.0\ \text{min} \times 60\ \text{s/min} = 3000\ \text{s}\). The charge passed is \(q = It = (9.65\ \text{A})(3000\ \text{s}) = 28{,}950\ \text{C}\), so the moles of electrons are \(n_{e^-} = q/F = 28{,}950 / 96{,}485 = 0.300\ \text{mol e}^-\). The half-reaction \(2\text{Cl}^- \rightarrow \text{Cl}_2 + 2e^-\) shows that \(2\) mol of electrons are released per mole of \(\text{Cl}_2\), so \(n_{\text{Cl}_2} = 0.300 / 2 = 0.150\ \text{mol}\). At STP, \(V = (0.150\ \text{mol})(22.4\ \text{L/mol}) = 3.36\ \text{L}\). (Choice B) \(6.72\ \text{L}\) results from multiplying the full \(0.300\ \text{mol e}^-\) by \(22.4\ \text{L/mol}\) directly, omitting the divisor of \(2\) electrons per \(\text{Cl}_2\) molecule. (Choice C) \(1.68\ \text{L}\) comes from dividing the moles of electrons by \(4\) instead of \(2\), the value appropriate for oxygen evolution (\(2\text{H}_2\text{O} \rightarrow \text{O}_2 + 4\text{H}^+ + 4e^-\)) rather than the \(2\)-electron chlorine-evolution half-reaction. (Choice D) \(0.0560\ \text{L}\) arises from a unit slip in which the time is used as \(50.0\ \text{s}\) without converting the \(50.0\ \text{min}\) to seconds, giving only \(q = 482.5\ \text{C}\). Skill 2$ex17$,
    'medium', '4C', $cc17$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc17$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'B', 'misconception', $a17B$Equates moles of gas with moles of electrons, omitting the electrons-per-molecule divisor from the half-reaction$a17B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a17C$Applies the 4-electron-per-molecule divisor of oxygen evolution to the 2-electron chlorine-evolution half-reaction$a17C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'scale_unit_error', $a17D$Fails to convert minutes to seconds before applying q = It, underscaling the charge by a factor of 60$a17D$ FROM q;

-- Q3 [Electrolysis & Faraday's Law · easy · Skill 1 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans A] (g45)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Electrolysis & Faraday''s Law',
    $qt45$In electrochemical calculations, the Faraday constant \(F\) is frequently used to relate charge to moles of electrons. Which of the following correctly states the value and physical meaning of \(F\)?$qt45$,
    $op45$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(F = 96{,}485\\ \\text{C/mol e}^-\\); it is the magnitude of electric charge carried by one mole of electrons."},{"label":"B","text":"\\(F = 96{,}485\\ \\text{J/mol}\\); it is the energy released when one mole of electrons is transferred."},{"label":"C","text":"\\(F = 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}\\ \\text{C}\\); it is the magnitude of electric charge carried by a single electron."},{"label":"D","text":"\\(F = 6.022 \\times 10^{23}\\ \\text{mol}^{-1}\\); it is the number of electrons in one mole."}]$op45$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex45$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is A because the Faraday constant is defined as the magnitude of electric charge per mole of electrons, \(F = 96{,}485\ \text{C/mol e}^-\). It is obtained by multiplying the elementary charge \((1.602 \times 10^{-19}\ \text{C per electron})\) by Avogadro's number \((6.022 \times 10^{23}\ \text{electrons/mol})\): \((1.602 \times 10^{-19}\ \text{C})\times(6.022 \times 10^{23}\ \text{mol}^{-1}) \approx 96{,}485\ \text{C/mol}\). This is exactly the constant used in relations such as \(q = nF\) and \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -nFE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\), where charge (in coulombs) is tied to moles of electrons. (Choice B) The value \(96{,}485\) is correct in magnitude, but \(F\) carries units of coulombs per mole (charge per mole), not joules per mole (energy per mole); \(F\) is a charge quantity, not an energy quantity. (Choice C) The value \(1.6 \times 10^{-19}\ \text{C}\) is the elementary charge of a single electron, not the charge of one mole of electrons; \(F\) refers to the charge of an entire mole, not one particle. (Choice D) The value \(6.022 \times 10^{23}\ \text{mol}^{-1}\) is Avogadro's number, the count of particles per mole; while it is used to derive \(F\), it is a pure count and is not itself the Faraday constant nor a charge. Skill 1$ex45$,
    'easy', '4C', $cc45$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc45$, 'general-chemistry', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'B', 'scale_unit_error', $a45B$Right magnitude, wrong units (charge labeled as energy)$a45B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'adjacent_fact', $a45C$Confuses elementary charge of one electron with charge of one mole$a45C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'misconception', $a45D$Confuses Faraday constant with Avogadro's number$a45D$ FROM q;

-- Q4 [Electrolysis & Faraday's Law · easy · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans B] (g46)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Electrolysis & Faraday''s Law',
    $qt46$A chemist electroplates silver from a solution of silver nitrate, where the relevant half-reaction is \(\text{Ag}^{+} + e^{-} \rightarrow \text{Ag}\). A constant current of \(2.00\ \text{A}\) is passed through the cell for \(30.0\ \text{min}\). Given the Faraday constant \(F = 96{,}485\ \text{C/mol e}^-\) and the molar mass of silver \(M(\text{Ag}) = 107.87\ \text{g/mol}\), what mass of silver is deposited at the cathode?$qt46$,
    $op46$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(241.5\\ \\text{g}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(4.03\\ \\text{g}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(0.0671\\ \\text{g}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(8.05\\ \\text{g}\\)"}]$op46$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex46$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is B because the mass deposited is found by chaining charge, moles of electrons, moles of metal, and mass. First convert time to seconds: \(t = 30.0\ \text{min} \times 60\ \text{s/min} = 1800\ \text{s}\). The total charge passed is \(q = It = (2.00\ \text{A})(1800\ \text{s}) = 3600\ \text{C}\). The moles of electrons are \(\frac{q}{F} = \frac{3600\ \text{C}}{96{,}485\ \text{C/mol e}^-} = 0.0373\ \text{mol e}^-\). Because the half-reaction \(\text{Ag}^{+} + e^{-} \rightarrow \text{Ag}\) has \(n = 1\), the moles of Ag equal the moles of electrons: \(0.0373\ \text{mol Ag}\). Multiplying by the molar mass gives \(0.0373\ \text{mol} \times 107.87\ \text{g/mol} = 4.03\ \text{g}\). (Choice A) \(241.5\ \text{g}\) results from applying the minute-to-second conversion in the wrong direction, multiplying the correct mass by 60 instead of incorporating the factor of 60 into the charge: \(4.025\ \text{g} \times 60 = 241.5\ \text{g}\). The factor of 60 belongs inside the charge calculation through \(t\), not as a final multiplier on the mass. (Choice C) \(0.0671\ \text{g}\) comes from leaving time in minutes (\(q = 2.00 \times 30.0 = 60\ \text{C}\)) rather than converting to seconds, undercounting the charge by a factor of 60. (Choice D) \(8.05\ \text{g}\) results from doubling the correct answer (\(2 \times 4.025\ \text{g} = 8.05\ \text{g}\)), as if one electron transferred deposited two silver atoms. The half-reaction \(\text{Ag}^{+} + e^{-} \rightarrow \text{Ag}\) shows a strict 1:1 ratio of electrons to silver atoms, so no factor of 2 enters; note that genuinely (and incorrectly) using \(n = 2\) would instead halve the result to \(2.01\ \text{g}\), the opposite of this inflation. Skill 2$ex46$,
    'easy', '4C', $cc46$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc46$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'scale_unit_error', $a46A$applies the factor-of-60 minute-second conversion as a final multiplier on mass instead of inside the charge$a46A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'scale_unit_error', $a46C$fails to convert minutes to seconds, undercounting total charge by 60$a46C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'misconception', $a46D$doubles the correct mass as if one electron deposits two silver atoms$a46D$ FROM q;

-- Q5 [Electrolysis & Faraday's Law · medium · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans C] (g47)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Electrolysis & Faraday''s Law',
    $qt47$An electrolytic cell is used to electroplate copper from an aqueous copper(II) sulfate (\(\text{CuSO}_4\)) solution. At the cathode, copper ions are reduced according to \(\text{Cu}^{2+} + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{Cu}\). A constant current of \(5.00\ \text{A}\) is passed through the cell for exactly \(1.00\ \text{hour}\). Given the Faraday constant \(F = 96{,}485\ \text{C/mol e}^-\) and the molar mass of copper \(M(\text{Cu}) = 63.55\ \text{g/mol}\), what mass of copper is deposited on the cathode?$qt47$,
    $op47$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(23.7\\ \\text{g}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(11.9\\ \\text{g}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(5.93\\ \\text{g}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(0.0988\\ \\text{g}\\)"}]$op47$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex47$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is C because the mass deposited is found by chaining charge, moles of electrons, moles of metal, and finally mass, taking care to divide by \(n = 2\) for the divalent ion. First, the total charge passed is \(q = It = (5.00\ \text{A})(1.00\ \text{hour} \times 3600\ \text{s/hour}) = 18{,}000\ \text{C}\). Next, the moles of electrons transferred are \(\frac{q}{F} = \frac{18{,}000\ \text{C}}{96{,}485\ \text{C/mol e}^-} = 0.1866\ \text{mol e}^-\). Because the half-reaction \(\text{Cu}^{2+} + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{Cu}\) requires \(2\) moles of electrons per mole of copper, the moles of copper deposited are \(\frac{0.1866\ \text{mol e}^-}{2} = 0.09328\ \text{mol Cu}\). Finally, the mass is \(0.09328\ \text{mol} \times 63.55\ \text{g/mol} = 5.93\ \text{g}\). (Choice A) \(23.7\ \text{g}\) results from multiplying by \(n = 2\) instead of dividing by it (\(0.1866 \times 2 \times 63.55\)); the stoichiometric factor must reduce the metal yield, not increase it. (Choice B) \(11.9\ \text{g}\) results from omitting the divalent factor, i.e., using \(n = 1\) and computing \(0.1866\ \text{mol} \times 63.55\ \text{g/mol}\); this fails to account for the two electrons required to reduce each \(\text{Cu}^{2+}\). (Choice D) \(0.0988\ \text{g}\) results from a unit error in the time, using \(t = 60\ \text{s}\) (treating "1 hour" as 60 seconds) rather than the correct \(3600\ \text{s}\), which underestimates the charge by a factor of 60. Skill 2$ex47$,
    'medium', '4C', $cc47$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc47$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a47A$multiplies by n=2 instead of dividing by it$a47A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'process_step_confusion', $a47B$omits the divalent factor and uses n=1 instead of n=2$a47B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'scale_unit_error', $a47D$treats 1 hour as 60 s instead of 3600 s$a47D$ FROM q;

-- Q6 [Electrolysis & Faraday's Law · medium · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans D] (g48)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Electrolysis & Faraday''s Law',
    $qt48$An electrolytic cell plates nickel onto a steel spoon from a solution of \(\text{Ni}^{2+}\) according to the half-reaction \(\text{Ni}^{2+} + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{Ni}\). A constant current of \(4.00\ \text{A}\) is passed through the cell. Assuming \(100\%\) current efficiency and that \(F = 96{,}485\ \text{C/mol e}^-\) and \(M(\text{Ni}) = 58.69\ \text{g/mol}\), approximately how long must the current flow to deposit \(2.00\ \text{g}\) of nickel?$qt48$,
    $op48$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(822\\ \\text{s}\\) (about 14 min)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(411\\ \\text{s}\\) (about 7 min)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(3{,}288\\ \\text{s}\\) (about 55 min)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(1{,}644\\ \\text{s}\\) (about 27 min)"}]$op48$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex48$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is D because the deposition is governed by Faraday's law, which links charge passed to moles of metal reduced. First find moles of Ni: \(n_{\text{Ni}} = \frac{2.00\ \text{g}}{58.69\ \text{g/mol}} = 0.0341\ \text{mol}\). Because each \(\text{Ni}^{2+}\) requires 2 electrons (\(\text{Ni}^{2+} + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{Ni}\)), the moles of electrons are \(0.0341 \times 2 = 0.0682\ \text{mol e}^-\). The charge required is \(q = (0.0682\ \text{mol e}^-)(96{,}485\ \text{C/mol e}^-) = 6{,}576\ \text{C}\). Finally, since \(q = It\), solve for time: \(t = \frac{q}{I} = \frac{6{,}576\ \text{C}}{4.00\ \text{A}} = 1{,}644\ \text{s}\), or about 27 min. (Choice A) \(822\ \text{s}\) results from omitting the factor of 2 for the divalent ion (treating Ni as if only 1 electron were needed), which halves the required charge and therefore the time. (Choice B) \(411\ \text{s}\) results from compounding the same one-electron error and an additional factor-of-2 mistake, giving one-quarter of the correct time. (Choice C) \(3{,}288\ \text{s}\) results from incorrectly multiplying by an extra factor of 2 (using \(n = 4\) electrons), doubling the correct time. Skill 2$ex48$,
    'medium', '4C', $cc48$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc48$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'process_step_confusion', $a48A$Omits the n = 2 electron factor for the divalent ion$a48A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'process_step_confusion', $a48B$Compounds the missing-electron error with an additional halving$a48B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'scale_unit_error', $a48C$Applies an extra factor of 2, doubling the required charge/time$a48C$ FROM q;

-- Q7 [Electrolysis & Faraday's Law · medium · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans A] (g49)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Electrolysis & Faraday''s Law',
    $qt49$An electrolytic cell is operated at a constant current of \(I = 3.00\ \text{A}\) for \(2.00\ \text{hours}\). Given Faraday's constant \(F = 96{,}485\ \text{C/mol e}^-\), how many moles of electrons pass through the cell during this time?$qt49$,
    $op49$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(0.224\\ \\text{mol e}^-\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(0.0622\\ \\text{mol e}^-\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(13.4\\ \\text{mol e}^-\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(0.448\\ \\text{mol e}^-\\)"}]$op49$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex49$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is A because the total charge passed is found from \(q = It\), where the time must first be converted to seconds: \(t = 2.00\ \text{hours} \times 3600\ \text{s/hour} = 7200\ \text{s}\). Then \(q = (3.00\ \text{A})(7200\ \text{s}) = 21{,}600\ \text{C}\) (recall that \(1\ \text{A} = 1\ \text{C/s}\)). Dividing the total charge by Faraday's constant converts coulombs to moles of electrons: \(n_{e^-} = \frac{q}{F} = \frac{21{,}600\ \text{C}}{96{,}485\ \text{C/mol e}^-} = 0.224\ \text{mol e}^-\). (Choice B) \(0.0622\ \text{mol e}^-\) results from converting hours to seconds with the wrong factor, treating \(1\ \text{hour} = 1000\ \text{s}\) so that \(t = 2000\ \text{s}\) and \(q = (3.00\ \text{A})(2000\ \text{s}) = 6000\ \text{C}\); dividing by \(F\) gives \(6000/96{,}485 = 0.0622\ \text{mol e}^-\), underestimating the true charge by the ratio \(3600/1000\). (Choice C) \(13.4\ \text{mol e}^-\) results from applying an extra, unnecessary factor of 60 to the conversion, for example computing the charge as \((3.00\ \text{A})(7200\ \text{s})(60) = 1{,}296{,}000\ \text{C}\) by treating the already-converted time as though it still needed a minutes-to-seconds step; dividing by \(F\) then yields \(1{,}296{,}000/96{,}485 = 13.4\ \text{mol e}^-\), which is exactly 60 times the correct value. (Choice D) \(0.448\ \text{mol e}^-\) is exactly twice the correct value and reflects erroneously introducing an \(n = 2\) electron factor, as though two electrons were required per unit charge; moles of electrons depend only on \(q/F\) and not on any species' charge number, so no such factor belongs in this calculation. Skill 2$ex49$,
    'medium', '4C', $cc49$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc49$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
