-- Q8 [Electrolysis & Faraday's Law · medium · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans B] (g50)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Electrolysis & Faraday''s Law',
    $qt50$In a Hall-Héroult-type process, aluminum is produced by the cathodic reduction \(\text{Al}^{3+} + 3e^- \rightarrow \text{Al}\). A constant current of \(10.0\ \text{A}\) is passed through the molten electrolyte for \(2.00\ \text{hours}\). Given Faraday's constant \(F = 96{,}485\ \text{C/mol e}^-\) and the molar mass of aluminum \(M = 26.98\ \text{g/mol}\), what mass of aluminum is deposited at the cathode?$qt50$,
    $op50$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(10.1\\ \\text{g}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(6.71\\ \\text{g}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(20.1\\ \\text{g}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(60.4\\ \\text{g}\\)"}]$op50$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex50$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is B because the moles of metal deposited follow \(n_{\text{Al}} = \frac{It}{nF}\), where \(n\) is the number of electrons transferred per metal ion. First convert the time to seconds: \(t = 2.00\ \text{h} \times 3600\ \text{s/h} = 7200\ \text{s}\). The total charge is \(q = It = (10.0\ \text{A})(7200\ \text{s}) = 72{,}000\ \text{C}\). Dividing by Faraday's constant gives moles of electrons: \(\frac{72{,}000\ \text{C}}{96{,}485\ \text{C/mol e}^-} = 0.7462\ \text{mol e}^-\). Because the reduction \(\text{Al}^{3+} + 3e^- \rightarrow \text{Al}\) requires \(n = 3\) electrons per aluminum atom, the moles of Al are \(\frac{0.7462\ \text{mol e}^-}{3} = 0.2487\ \text{mol Al}\). Multiplying by the molar mass: \(0.2487\ \text{mol} \times 26.98\ \text{g/mol} = 6.71\ \text{g}\). (Choice A) \(10.1\ \text{g}\) results from using \(n = 2\) (treating aluminum as a divalent ion) instead of the correct \(n = 3\), giving \(0.7462/2 = 0.373\ \text{mol Al}\); the half-reaction explicitly transfers three electrons. (Choice C) \(20.1\ \text{g}\) results from omitting the electron count entirely (\(n = 1\)), incorrectly setting moles of Al equal to moles of electrons (\(0.7462\ \text{mol}\)); this ignores that each \(\text{Al}^{3+}\) requires three electrons. (Choice D) \(60.4\ \text{g}\) results from multiplying by \(n = 3\) rather than dividing by it, inverting the role of the electron stoichiometry in \(n_{\text{Al}} = It/(nF)\). Skill 2$ex50$,
    'medium', '4C', $cc50$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc50$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
