-- Q9 [Electrolysis & Faraday's Law · medium · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans C] (g51)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Electrolysis & Faraday''s Law',
    $qt51$A student wires two electrolytic cells in series so that the same total charge passes through both. The first cell contains aqueous \(\text{Ag}^{+}\) and the second contains aqueous \(\text{Cu}^{2+}\); both metals plate out at their respective cathodes via \(\text{Ag}^{+} + e^{-} \rightarrow \text{Ag}\) and \(\text{Cu}^{2+} + 2e^{-} \rightarrow \text{Cu}\). After a total charge of \(9{,}650\ \text{C}\) has passed through the series, exactly \(0.100\ \text{mol}\) of silver has been deposited in the first cell. Using \(F = 96{,}500\ \text{C/mol e}^{-}\), how many moles of copper are deposited in the second cell?$qt51$,
    $op51$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(0.200\\ \\text{mol}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(0.100\\ \\text{mol}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(0.0500\\ \\text{mol}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(0.0250\\ \\text{mol}\\)"}]$op51$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex51$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is C because the same total charge delivers the same number of moles of electrons to each cell, and the moles of metal deposited equal the moles of electrons divided by the ionic charge \(n\). The charge fixes the electrons: \(\text{mol } e^{-} = \frac{q}{F} = \frac{9{,}650\ \text{C}}{96{,}500\ \text{C/mol}} = 0.100\ \text{mol } e^{-}\). For silver, \(\text{Ag}^{+} + e^{-} \rightarrow \text{Ag}\) needs \(1\ e^{-}\) per atom, so \(0.100\ \text{mol } e^{-}\) gives \(0.100\ \text{mol Ag}\), matching the stated value and confirming the electron count. For copper, \(\text{Cu}^{2+} + 2e^{-} \rightarrow \text{Cu}\) needs \(2\ e^{-}\) per atom, so \(\text{mol Cu} = \frac{0.100\ \text{mol } e^{-}}{2} = 0.0500\ \text{mol}\). The same charge therefore deposits only half as much copper (in moles) as silver because each \(\text{Cu}^{2+}\) consumes twice the electrons of each \(\text{Ag}^{+}\). (Choice A) \(0.200\ \text{mol}\) inverts the relationship by multiplying the electron moles by \(n = 2\) instead of dividing, treating higher ionic charge as producing more metal rather than less. (Choice B) \(0.100\ \text{mol}\) wrongly assumes copper is deposited in the same molar amount as silver, ignoring that \(\text{Cu}^{2+}\) requires \(2\ e^{-}\) per atom rather than \(1\). (Choice D) \(0.0250\ \text{mol}\) applies the charge-division correction twice (dividing \(0.100\) by \(2\) and again by \(2\)), an extra processing step that overcorrects for the \(2+\) charge. Skill 2$ex51$,
    'medium', '4C', $cc51$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc51$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
