UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'misconception', $a51B$Assumes equal moles deposited regardless of ionic charge, ignoring that Cu2+ needs 2 electrons per atom$a51B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a51D$Applies the divide-by-2 charge correction twice, overcorrecting for the 2+ ion$a51D$ FROM q;

-- Q10 [Electrolysis & Faraday's Law · hard · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans D] (g52)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Electrolysis & Faraday''s Law',
    $qt52$A manufacturer electroplates a steel part with chromium metal from a bath containing \(\text{Cr}^{3+}\) ions, where the cathode half-reaction is \(\text{Cr}^{3+} + 3e^- \rightarrow \text{Cr}(s)\). A constant current of \(6.00\ \text{A}\) is passed through the cell, and the goal is to deposit \(5.20\ \text{g}\) of chromium onto the part. Given that the molar mass of chromium is \(52.00\ \text{g/mol}\) and the Faraday constant is \(F = 96{,}485\ \text{C/mol e}^-\), and assuming \(100\%\) current efficiency, approximately how long must the current be applied?$qt52$,
    $op52$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(4824\\ \\text{min}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(26.8\\ \\text{min}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(8.93\\ \\text{min}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(80.4\\ \\text{min}\\)"}]$op52$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex52$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is D because the deposition is governed by Faraday's law, which links the charge passed to the moles of electrons and therefore the moles (and mass) of metal plated. First find the moles of chromium: \(n_{\text{Cr}} = \frac{5.20\ \text{g}}{52.00\ \text{g/mol}} = 0.100\ \text{mol}\). Because the half-reaction \(\text{Cr}^{3+} + 3e^- \rightarrow \text{Cr}\) requires 3 electrons per chromium atom, the moles of electrons are \(n_{e^-} = 3 \times 0.100 = 0.300\ \text{mol e}^-\). Convert to charge with the Faraday constant: \(q = n_{e^-} \times F = 0.300\ \text{mol e}^- \times 96{,}485\ \text{C/mol e}^- \approx 2.89 \times 10^4\ \text{C}\) (28,946 C). Then use \(q = It\) to solve for time: \(t = \frac{q}{I} = \frac{28{,}946\ \text{C}}{6.00\ \text{A}} \approx 4824\ \text{s}\). Converting seconds to minutes, \(t = \frac{4824\ \text{s}}{60\ \text{s/min}} \approx 80.4\ \text{min}\), which matches choice D. (Choice A) \(4824\ \text{min}\) is the correctly computed time in seconds mislabeled as minutes; it fails to convert seconds to minutes (dividing by 60), an error of unit/scale. (Choice B) \(26.8\ \text{min}\) results from omitting the factor of 3 and treating the deposition as a one-electron process (using \(n_{e^-} = 0.100\ \text{mol}\) instead of \(0.300\ \text{mol}\)), which ignores that \(\text{Cr}^{3+}\) requires three electrons per atom. (Choice C) \(8.93\ \text{min}\) results from incorrectly dividing the moles of chromium by 3 rather than multiplying, reversing the role of the electron stoichiometry in the conversion. Skill 2$ex52$,
    'hard', '4C', $cc52$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc52$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
