UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'process_step_confusion', $a52B$Drops the n=3 electron factor, treating Cr3+ plating as a one-electron deposition$a52B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'reversed_relationship', $a52C$Divides moles of metal by n instead of multiplying, inverting the electron stoichiometry$a52C$ FROM q;

-- Q11 [Electrolysis & Faraday's Law · hard · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans A] (g53)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Electrolysis & Faraday''s Law',
    $qt53$Two electrolytic cells are wired in series, so the same total charge passes through both. The first cell contains \(\text{Ag}^{+}\) ions and plates out silver by \(\text{Ag}^{+} + e^{-} \rightarrow \text{Ag}\). The second cell contains \(\text{Au}^{3+}\) ions and plates out gold by \(\text{Au}^{3+} + 3e^{-} \rightarrow \text{Au}\). After the current runs, exactly \(2.16\ \text{g}\) of silver has deposited on the cathode of the first cell. Using molar masses \(M(\text{Ag}) = 107.87\ \text{g/mol}\) and \(M(\text{Au}) = 196.97\ \text{g/mol}\) and the Faraday constant \(F = 96{,}485\ \text{C/mol e}^{-}\), what mass of gold is deposited in the second cell?$qt53$,
    $op53$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(1.31\\ \\text{g}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(3.94\\ \\text{g}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(11.8\\ \\text{g}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(1.97\\ \\text{g}\\)"}]$op53$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex53$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is A because cells in series pass identical charge, so they pass identical moles of electrons; the metals differ only in how many electrons each ion needs. First find moles of electrons from the silver: \(\text{Ag}^{+} + e^{-} \rightarrow \text{Ag}\) is a 1-electron reduction, so \(\text{mol Ag} = \frac{2.16\ \text{g}}{107.87\ \text{g/mol}} = 0.0200\ \text{mol}\), and \(\text{mol } e^{-} = 0.0200\ \text{mol}\) (this corresponds to \(q = It = (0.0200)(96{,}485) = 1.93\times10^{3}\ \text{C}\), the same charge through the gold cell). Gold requires 3 electrons per atom: \(\text{Au}^{3+} + 3e^{-} \rightarrow \text{Au}\), so \(\text{mol Au} = \frac{\text{mol } e^{-}}{3} = \frac{0.0200}{3} = 6.67\times10^{-3}\ \text{mol}\). Therefore \(\text{mass Au} = (6.67\times10^{-3}\ \text{mol})(196.97\ \text{g/mol}) = 1.31\ \text{g}\). (Choice B) \(3.94\ \text{g}\) results from skipping the division by \(n = 3\) and treating gold as a 1-electron deposit (equivalently, deposits the same number of moles of metal as silver), which ignores that each \(\text{Au}^{3+}\) consumes three electrons. (Choice C) \(11.8\ \text{g}\) reverses the electron-count step by multiplying the moles of electrons by 3 instead of dividing, inverting the relationship between charge needed and atoms deposited. (Choice D) \(1.97\ \text{g}\) uses \(n = 2\) for gold rather than the correct \(n = 3\), an error in the electron count taken from the half-reaction. Skill 2$ex53$,
    'hard', '4C', $cc53$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc53$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
