-- Q12 [Electrolysis & Faraday's Law · hard · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans B] (g54)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Electrolysis & Faraday''s Law',
    $qt54$A constant current of \(8.00\ \text{A}\) is passed through an electrolytic cell containing dilute aqueous \(\text{Na}_2\text{SO}_4\) for \(20.0\ \text{min}\). At the cathode, water is reduced according to \(2\text{H}_2\text{O}(l) + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{H}_2(g) + 2\text{OH}^-(aq)\). Assuming \(100\%\) current efficiency, \(F = 96{,}485\ \text{C/mol e}^-\), and that the liberated gas is collected at STP where the molar volume is \(22.4\ \text{L/mol}\), what volume of \(\text{H}_2\) gas is evolved at the cathode?$qt54$,
    $op54$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(2.23\\ \\text{L}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(1.11\\ \\text{L}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(0.557\\ \\text{L}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(0.0186\\ \\text{L}\\)"}]$op54$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex54$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is B because the volume follows from a chain of Faraday's law, the cathode half-reaction stoichiometry, and the molar gas volume. First find the total charge: \(q = It = (8.00\ \text{A})(20.0\ \text{min} \times 60\ \text{s/min}) = (8.00)(1200) = 9600\ \text{C}\). Convert charge to moles of electrons: \(n_{e^-} = q/F = 9600 / 96{,}485 = 0.0995\ \text{mol e}^-\). The cathode half-reaction \(2\text{H}_2\text{O} + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{H}_2 + 2\text{OH}^-\) requires \(2\) electrons per \(\text{H}_2\) molecule, so \(n_{\text{H}_2} = n_{e^-}/2 = 0.0995/2 = 0.0498\ \text{mol H}_2\). Finally, applying the STP molar volume: \(V = (0.0498\ \text{mol})(22.4\ \text{L/mol}) = 1.11\ \text{L}\). (Choice A) \(2.23\ \text{L}\) results from omitting the \(n = 2\) factor and equating moles of electrons directly with moles of \(\text{H}_2\) (a 1:1 ratio), i.e., \(0.0995 \times 22.4\); this ignores that each \(\text{H}_2\) requires two electrons. (Choice C) \(0.557\ \text{L}\) results from dividing by an extra factor of 2, applying the electron count a second time (treating \(n\) as 4 overall) so that \(n_{\text{H}_2} = 0.0995/4\); only one division by 2 is warranted. (Choice D) \(0.0186\ \text{L}\) results from leaving the time in minutes rather than converting to seconds, giving \(q = (8.00)(20.0) = 160\ \text{C}\) and propagating that error through the same steps; the ampere is coulombs per second, so time must be in seconds. Skill 2$ex54$,
    'hard', '4C', $cc54$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc54$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
