-- Q13 [Free Energy & Cell Potential · easy · Skill 1 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans C] (g55)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Free Energy & Cell Potential',
    $qt55$A galvanic cell is assembled from two half-cells under standard conditions and operates spontaneously with a standard cell potential of \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = +0.45\ \text{V}\), transferring \(n = 2\) moles of electrons per mole of reaction. Using \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -nFE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) with \(F = 96{,}485\ \text{C/mol e}^-\), which of the following correctly reports the value and sign of the standard Gibbs free energy change, \(\Delta G^{\circ}\), for the cell reaction as written?$qt55$,
    $op55$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\Delta G^{\\circ} \\approx +87\\ \\text{kJ}\\) (positive), because the cell is galvanic"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\Delta G^{\\circ} = 0\\) (zero), because a galvanic cell operates at equilibrium"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\Delta G^{\\circ} \\approx -87\\ \\text{kJ}\\) (negative), because the cell is galvanic (spontaneous) and \\(E^{\\circ}_{\\text{cell}} > 0\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"The sign of \\(\\Delta G^{\\circ}\\) cannot be determined without knowing the ion concentrations in each half-cell"}]$op55$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex55$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is C because the cell is galvanic and runs spontaneously with a positive standard cell potential, \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = +0.45\ \text{V}\), so substituting into \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -nFE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) gives \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -(2)(96{,}485\ \text{C/mol e}^-)(+0.45\ \text{V}) = -86{,}836.5\ \text{J} \approx -86.8\ \text{kJ}\); the leading negative sign acting on positive values of \(n\), \(F\), and \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) forces \(\Delta G^{\circ}\) to be negative, consistent with a spontaneous process. (Choice A) The magnitude \(\approx 87\ \text{kJ}\) is correct, but the sign is reversed: a positive \(\Delta G^{\circ}\) corresponds to a nonspontaneous (electrolytic) reaction, whereas a spontaneous galvanic cell with \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} > 0\) must give a negative \(\Delta G^{\circ}\). (Choice B) A value of exactly zero would require \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = 0\) (a system at equilibrium delivering no net work), but a galvanic cell with \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = +0.45\ \text{V}\) is displaced from equilibrium and does net work, so \(\Delta G^{\circ} \neq 0\). (Choice D) The sign and value of the standard free energy change follow directly from the standard cell potential through \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -nFE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\); standard conditions already fix all activities at unity (1 M for solutes), so no additional concentration information is needed. Skill 1$ex55$,
    'easy', '4C', $cc55$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc55$, 'general-chemistry', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
