-- Q14 [Free Energy & Cell Potential · easy · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans D] (g56)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Free Energy & Cell Potential',
    $qt56$The Daniell cell pairs a zinc electrode with a copper electrode according to the overall reaction \(\text{Zn}(s) + \text{Cu}^{2+}(aq) \rightarrow \text{Zn}^{2+}(aq) + \text{Cu}(s)\), with a standard cell potential of \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = +1.10\ \text{V}\) and \(n = 2\) electrons transferred. Using \(F = 96{,}485\ \text{C/mol e}^-\), what is the standard free energy change \(\Delta G^{\circ}\) for this cell?$qt56$,
    $op56$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(-2.12 \\times 10^{2}\\ \\text{J}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(+2.12 \\times 10^{5}\\ \\text{J}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(-1.06 \\times 10^{5}\\ \\text{J}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(-2.12 \\times 10^{5}\\ \\text{J}\\)"}]$op56$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex56$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is D because the standard free energy change is found from \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -nFE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\). Substituting the given values, \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -(2)(96{,}485\ \text{C/mol})(1.10\ \text{V})\). First, \(96{,}485 \times 1.10 = 106{,}133.5\), and then \(\times 2 = 212{,}267\), so \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -212{,}267\ \text{J} \approx -2.12 \times 10^{5}\ \text{J}\). The negative sign is required and physically correct: a galvanic cell with \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} > 0\) is spontaneous, so \(\Delta G^{\circ} < 0\). (Choice A) \(-2.12 \times 10^{2}\ \text{J}\) is the answer expressed in kilojoules (\(-212\ \text{kJ}\)) but mislabeled as joules, an error of a factor of \(10^{3}\); since \(F\) is given in coulombs per mole, the product \(nFE^{\circ}\) is already in joules and no division by 1000 is warranted. (Choice B) \(+2.12 \times 10^{5}\ \text{J}\) has the correct magnitude but drops the leading negative sign of the relationship \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -nFE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\); a positive \(\Delta G^{\circ}\) would imply a nonspontaneous cell, contradicting the positive cell potential of a galvanic cell. (Choice C) \(-1.06 \times 10^{5}\ \text{J}\) results from using \(n = 1\) instead of \(n = 2\); the balanced reaction transfers two electrons (\(\text{Zn} \rightarrow \text{Zn}^{2+} + 2e^-\)), so the factor of \(n = 2\) must be included. Skill 2$ex56$,
    'easy', '4C', $cc56$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc56$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
