-- Q15 [Free Energy & Cell Potential · medium · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans A] (g57)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Free Energy & Cell Potential',
    $qt57$A galvanic cell is constructed from a metal/metal-ion couple that delivers a standard cell potential of \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = +0.62\ \text{V}\), and the balanced overall reaction transfers \(n = 2\) moles of electrons. Using the Faraday constant \(F = 96{,}485\ \text{C/mol e}^-\) and the relationship \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -nFE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\), what is the standard free energy change for this cell reaction as written?$qt57$,
    $op57$[{"label":"A","text":"approximately \\(-120\\ \\text{kJ}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"approximately \\(+120\\ \\text{kJ}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"approximately \\(-60\\ \\text{kJ}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"approximately \\(-240\\ \\text{kJ}\\)"}]$op57$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex57$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is A because \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -nFE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = -(2)(96{,}485\ \text{C/mol})(0.62\ \text{V}) = -119{,}641\ \text{J} \approx -120\ \text{kJ}\). The product \(nF\) carries units of coulombs (charge), and multiplying by a potential in volts (joules per coulomb) yields joules; because \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) is positive the cell is spontaneous, so the leading negative sign in the formula correctly returns a negative (favorable) free energy change. (Choice B) A value of \(+120\ \text{kJ}\) has the right magnitude but the wrong sign; it results from dropping the negative sign in \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -nFE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\), which would incorrectly imply that this spontaneous galvanic reaction is nonspontaneous. (Choice C) A value of \(-60\ \text{kJ}\) comes from using \(n = 1\) instead of the \(n = 2\) electrons that are actually transferred, halving the true magnitude. (Choice D) A value of approximately \(-240\ \text{kJ}\) results from using too large an electron count (e.g., \(n = 4\)), roughly doubling the correct magnitude; the balanced reaction transfers only \(2\) moles of electrons. Skill 2$ex57$,
    'medium', '4C', $cc57$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc57$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
