-- Q16 [Free Energy & Cell Potential · medium · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans B] (g58)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Free Energy & Cell Potential',
    $qt58$A galvanic cell operating at 298 K has a standard cell potential of \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = +1.20\ \text{V}\) and a standard free energy change of \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -463\ \text{kJ}\). Using \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -nFE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) with \(F = 96{,}485\ \text{C/mol e}^-\), how many moles of electrons are transferred per mole of reaction as written?$qt58$,
    $op58$[{"label":"A","text":"2 mol e⁻"},{"label":"B","text":"4 mol e⁻"},{"label":"C","text":"8 mol e⁻"},{"label":"D","text":"3 mol e⁻"}]$op58$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex58$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is B because rearranging \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -nFE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) gives \(n = \dfrac{-\Delta G^{\circ}}{F\,E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}}\). First convert the free energy to joules so the units match the faraday constant: \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -463\ \text{kJ} = -463{,}000\ \text{J}\). Then \(n = \dfrac{-(-463{,}000\ \text{J})}{(96{,}485\ \text{C/mol e}^-)(1.20\ \text{V})} = \dfrac{463{,}000}{115{,}782} \approx 3.999\), which rounds to the nearest whole electron count of \(4\ \text{mol e}^-\). The double negative correctly yields a positive \(n\), consistent with a spontaneous galvanic cell having \(\Delta G^{\circ} < 0\) and \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} > 0\). (Choice A) Obtaining \(2\) requires doubling the cell potential to \(2.40\ \text{V}\) in the denominator, as if the \(1.20\ \text{V}\) had to be split between two half-cells; \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) is already the full cell value and must not be partitioned that way. (Choice C) Obtaining \(8\) comes from multiplying the correct result by two, as if each half-reaction's electrons were counted separately and then summed; \(n\) is the single number of electrons transferred in the balanced overall reaction, not twice that. (Choice D) Obtaining \(3\) results from truncating the computed value of \(3.999\) downward instead of rounding to the nearest whole number; because electron transfer must be an integer and \(3.999\) is essentially \(4\), the value rounds up. Skill 2$ex58$,
    'medium', '4C', $cc58$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc58$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
