-- Q17 [Free Energy & Cell Potential · medium · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans C] (g59)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Free Energy & Cell Potential',
    $qt59$A spontaneous galvanic cell carries out a balanced redox reaction whose standard free energy change is \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -210.0\ \text{kJ}\), and the balanced equation transfers \(n = 3\) moles of electrons per mole of reaction. Using the relationship \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -nFE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) with the Faraday constant \(F = 96{,}485\ \text{C/mol e}^-\), what is the standard cell potential \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) for this cell?$qt59$,
    $op59$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(-0.726\\ \\text{V}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(+0.242\\ \\text{V}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(+0.726\\ \\text{V}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(+7.26 \\times 10^{-4}\\ \\text{V}\\)"}]$op59$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex59$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The relationship between standard free energy and standard cell potential is \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -nFE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\), so to find the potential from a given free energy change it is rearranged to \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = -\Delta G^{\circ}/(nF)\). The answer to this question is C because the free energy must first be converted from kilojoules to joules, \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -210.0\ \text{kJ} = -210{,}000\ \text{J}\), and then substituted along with \(n = 3\) and \(F = 96{,}485\ \text{C/mol e}^-\): \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = -(-210{,}000\ \text{J})/[(3)(96{,}485\ \text{C/mol e}^-)] = 210{,}000/289{,}455 = +0.726\ \text{V}\). The positive sign is consistent with a spontaneous galvanic cell, which must have \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} > 0\) whenever \(\Delta G^{\circ} < 0\). (Choice A) This value, \(-0.726\ \text{V}\), comes from carrying the negative sign of \(\Delta G^{\circ}\) straight through without applying the negative sign in \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = -\Delta G^{\circ}/(nF)\); a negative potential would describe a non-spontaneous (electrolytic) process, which contradicts the stated spontaneous galvanic cell. (Choice B) This value, \(+0.242\ \text{V}\), results from dividing by the electron count twice (dividing by \(n^2 = 9\) rather than \(n = 3\)), i.e. \(210{,}000/[(9)(96{,}485)]\); the factor of \(n\) appears only once in \(nF\). (Choice D) This value, \(+7.26 \times 10^{-4}\ \text{V}\), comes from failing to convert kilojoules to joules and dividing \(210\ \text{J}\) (instead of \(210{,}000\ \text{J}\)) by \(nF\), an error of a factor of \(1000\). Skill 2$ex59$,
    'medium', '4C', $cc59$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc59$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
