-- Q18 [Free Energy & Cell Potential · hard · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans D] (g60)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Free Energy & Cell Potential',
    $qt60$Acidified permanganate oxidizes iron(II) according to the balanced reaction \(\text{MnO}_4^- + 5\,\text{Fe}^{2+} + 8\,\text{H}^+ \rightarrow \text{Mn}^{2+} + 5\,\text{Fe}^{3+} + 4\,\text{H}_2\text{O}\). This process can be run as a galvanic cell in which the half-reactions are \(\text{MnO}_4^- + 8\,\text{H}^+ + 5e^- \rightarrow \text{Mn}^{2+} + 4\,\text{H}_2\text{O}\) (\(E^{\circ} = +1.51\ \text{V}\)) and \(\text{Fe}^{3+} + e^- \rightarrow \text{Fe}^{2+}\) (\(E^{\circ} = +0.77\ \text{V}\)). Taking \(F = 96{,}485\ \text{C/mol e}^-\) and using \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -nFE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\), what is \(\Delta G^{\circ}\) for the balanced reaction as written, most nearly?$qt60$,
    $op60$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(-1100\\ \\text{kJ}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(-71\\ \\text{kJ}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(+357\\ \\text{kJ}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(-357\\ \\text{kJ}\\)"}]$op60$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex60$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is D because the calculation must chain three steps — deduce the electron count \(n\) from the balanced cell, compute \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) by subtraction, then convert to \(\Delta G^{\circ}\) in kilojoules. First, identify the electrodes: the species with the larger reduction potential is reduced, so permanganate (\(+1.51\ \text{V}\)) is the cathode and the iron couple (\(+0.77\ \text{V}\)) is the anode, where \(\text{Fe}^{2+}\) is oxidized to \(\text{Fe}^{3+}\). Although the iron half-reaction transfers only one electron per iron, the balanced overall reaction multiplies it by \(5\) to match the five electrons accepted by one permanganate; the number of electrons transferred per turnover of the balanced equation is therefore \(n = 5\), not \(1\). Next, \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = E^{\circ}_{\text{red,cathode}} - E^{\circ}_{\text{red,anode}} = 1.51\ \text{V} - 0.77\ \text{V} = +0.74\ \text{V}\); the positive value confirms a spontaneous galvanic cell (\(\Delta G^{\circ} < 0\)). Note that \(E^{\circ}\) is intensive and is never multiplied by the stoichiometric coefficient. Finally, \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -nFE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = -(5)(96{,}485\ \text{C/mol})(0.74\ \text{V}) = -356{,}994.5\ \text{J} \approx -357{,}000\ \text{J} = -357\ \text{kJ}\). (Choice A) \(-1100\ \text{kJ}\) results from adding the two reduction potentials, \(1.51 + 0.77 = 2.28\ \text{V}\), instead of subtracting them; \(-(5)(96{,}485)(2.28) \approx -1.10\times10^{6}\ \text{J} = -1100\ \text{kJ}\) reflects the misconception that \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) is the sum of the half-cell potentials. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must extract the electron count from the balanced equation, apply the subtractive convention for \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\), and carry the result through the \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -nFE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) relationship with correct sign and units. (Choice B) \(-71\ \text{kJ}\) uses \(n = 1\), the electrons transferred per iron in the unscaled \(\text{Fe}^{3+}/\text{Fe}^{2+}\) half-reaction, rather than the \(n = 5\) electrons transferred per balanced reaction; \(-(1)(96{,}485)(0.74) = -71{,}400\ \text{J} = -71\ \text{kJ}\), undercounting the electrons by a factor of five. (Choice C) \(+357\ \text{kJ}\) has the correct magnitude but the wrong sign: it comes from computing the cell potential as \(E^{\circ}_{\text{red,anode}} - E^{\circ}_{\text{red,cathode}} = 0.77 - 1.51 = -0.74\ \text{V}\), which yields \(\Delta G^{\circ} > 0\) and incorrectly labels a spontaneous galvanic reaction as nonspontaneous. Skill 2$ex60$,
    'hard', '4C', $cc60$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc60$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a60A$Adds the two reduction potentials (1.51 + 0.77 = 2.28 V) rather than subtracting them to get E°cell.$a60A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'process_step_confusion', $a60B$Uses n = 1 (the electrons per iron in the unscaled half-reaction) instead of n = 5 for the balanced cell, undercounting electrons fivefold.$a60B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'reversed_relationship', $a60C$Computes E°cell as anode minus cathode (0.77 - 1.51 = -0.74 V), flipping the sign of ΔG° and mislabeling the spontaneous cell as nonspontaneous.$a60C$ FROM q;

-- Q19 [Free Energy & Cell Potential · hard · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans A] (g61)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Free Energy & Cell Potential',
    $qt61$A dichromate/chromium(III) galvanic cell is described by the balanced reaction \(\text{Cr}_2\text{O}_7^{2-} + 14\,\text{H}^+ + 6\,\text{Fe}^{2+} \rightarrow 2\,\text{Cr}^{3+} + 6\,\text{Fe}^{3+} + 7\,\text{H}_2\text{O}\), for which \(n = 6\) and \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = +0.79\ \text{V}\). Two students are asked to find \(\Delta G^{\circ}\) for the same cell reaction written with every stoichiometric coefficient doubled, so that the balanced equation now transfers \(n = 12\). The first student doubles only \(n\) in \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -nFE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\); the second student reasons that doubling the equation must also double the cell potential and so uses \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = 1.58\ \text{V}\) together with \(n = 12\). Taking \(F = 96{,}485\ \text{C/mol e}^-\), what is the correct \(\Delta G^{\circ}\) for the doubled equation, and by what factor is the second student's value too large in magnitude?$qt61$,
    $op61$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\Delta G^{\\circ} \\approx -915\\ \\text{kJ}\\); the second student's value is too large by a factor of \\(2\\)."},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\Delta G^{\\circ} \\approx -915\\ \\text{kJ}\\); the second student's value is too large by a factor of \\(4\\)."},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\Delta G^{\\circ} \\approx -457\\ \\text{kJ}\\); the second student's value is too large by a factor of \\(2\\)."},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\Delta G^{\\circ} \\approx -1829\\ \\text{kJ}\\); the second student's value is too large by a factor of \\(2\\)."}]$op61$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex61$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is A because the correct value requires recognizing that \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) is an intensive property while \(\Delta G^{\circ}\) is extensive, then carrying out the \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -nFE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) calculation and a comparative ratio. When the balanced equation is doubled, the number of electrons transferred doubles from \(n = 6\) to \(n = 12\), but the cell potential is a per-electron (intensive) quantity that does NOT depend on how the equation is written, so \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) stays \(+0.79\ \text{V}\). The first student is therefore correct: \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -nFE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = -(12)(96{,}485\ \text{C/mol})(0.79\ \text{V}) = -914{,}678\ \text{J} \approx -915\ \text{kJ}\). The second student multiplied by an extra factor of \(2\) by also doubling \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) to \(1.58\ \text{V}\): \(-(12)(96{,}485)(1.58) = -1{,}829{,}356\ \text{J} \approx -1829\ \text{kJ}\). Comparing the erroneous value to the correct one, \(\frac{-1829\ \text{kJ}}{-915\ \text{kJ}} = 2\), so the second student's value is too large in magnitude by a factor of exactly \(2\). (Choice B) \(-915\ \text{kJ}\) is the correct \(\Delta G^{\circ}\), but the factor of \(4\) comes from comparing the second student's value against the original (undoubled) reaction's \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -(6)(96{,}485)(0.79) = -457\ \text{kJ}\) rather than against the doubled equation actually asked about; \(-1829/-457 = 4\) uses the wrong baseline. (Choice C) \(-457\ \text{kJ}\) is the \(\Delta G^{\circ}\) of the original \(n = 6\) equation; it fails to double \(n\) for the doubled equation at all, even though the factor of \(2\) for the second student happens to be stated correctly. (Choice D) \(-1829\ \text{kJ}\) reverses which student is right by treating the second student's double-counting (doubling both \(n\) and \(E^{\circ}\)) as the correct procedure, which mistakes the intensive \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) for an extensive quantity. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the intensive-versus-extensive distinction to decide what changes when an equation is scaled, execute the \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -nFE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) computation, and form a comparative ratio against the correct reference. Skill 2$ex61$,
    'hard', '4C', $cc61$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc61$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'B', 'process_step_confusion', $a61B$Correct ΔG° but computes the too-large factor against the original n=6 reaction (−1829/−457 = 4) instead of against the doubled equation that was asked about.$a61B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'partial_truth', $a61C$Keeps n = 6 and reports the original equation's ΔG° (−457 kJ), failing to double n for the doubled equation even though the factor of 2 is stated correctly.$a61C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'reversed_relationship', $a61D$Treats E°cell as extensive and adopts the second student's doubled-both result (−1829 kJ) as correct, reversing which student applied the proper procedure.$a61D$ FROM q;

-- Q20 [Cell Potential & Equilibrium · easy · Skill 1 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans B] (g62)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Cell Potential & Equilibrium',
    $qt62$For a certain redox reaction at 298 K, the equilibrium constant is \(K_{eq} = 2.5 \times 10^{3}\). Recall that the standard cell potential is related to the equilibrium constant by \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -nFE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = -RT\ln K_{eq}\), where \(n\), \(F\), \(R\), and \(T\) are all positive. Based on this value of \(K_{eq}\), what is the sign of \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\), and is the reaction spontaneous as written?$qt62$,
    $op62$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(E^{\\circ}_{\\text{cell}}\\) is negative, and the reaction is nonspontaneous as written."},{"label":"B","text":"\\(E^{\\circ}_{\\text{cell}}\\) is positive, and the reaction is spontaneous as written."},{"label":"C","text":"\\(E^{\\circ}_{\\text{cell}}\\) is positive, and the reaction is nonspontaneous as written."},{"label":"D","text":"\\(E^{\\circ}_{\\text{cell}}\\) is zero, and the reaction is at equilibrium as written."}]$op62$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex62$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is B because \(K_{eq} = 2.5 \times 10^{3}\) is greater than 1, so \(\ln K_{eq} > 0\). From \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -RT\ln K_{eq}\), a positive \(\ln K_{eq}\) makes \(\Delta G^{\circ} < 0\), which means the reaction is spontaneous as written. Combining the two relationships, \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -nFE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\); since \(\Delta G^{\circ} < 0\) and \(n\) and \(F\) are positive, \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) must be positive. So \(K_{eq} > 1 \Rightarrow \ln K_{eq} > 0 \Rightarrow \Delta G^{\circ} < 0 \Rightarrow E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} > 0\), and a positive \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) is the hallmark of a spontaneous (galvanic) reaction as written. (Choice A) This choice reverses every sign: it would correspond to \(K_{eq} < 1\) (i.e., \(\ln K_{eq} < 0\)), giving \(\Delta G^{\circ} > 0\) and \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} < 0\), which contradicts \(K_{eq} = 2.5 \times 10^{3} > 1\). (Choice C) This choice correctly identifies \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) as positive but then mislabels the reaction as nonspontaneous; a positive standard cell potential corresponds to \(\Delta G^{\circ} < 0\), which is spontaneous, so the two halves of this choice are inconsistent. (Choice D) This choice would be true only if \(K_{eq} = 1\) (so \(\ln K_{eq} = 0\) and \(\Delta G^{\circ} = E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = 0\)); because \(K_{eq} = 2.5 \times 10^{3} \neq 1\), the reaction mixture is not at standard-state equilibrium and \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) is not zero. Skill 1$ex62$,
    'easy', '4C', $cc62$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc62$, 'general-chemistry', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a62A$Flips the Keq-to-sign logic, treating Keq > 1 as if it were Keq < 1 (negative E and nonspontaneous)$a62A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'partial_truth', $a62C$Correct sign of E but inconsistently calls a positive-potential reaction nonspontaneous$a62C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'misconception', $a62D$Confuses any stated Keq with the at-equilibrium condition (Keq = 1, E = 0)$a62D$ FROM q;

-- Q21 [Cell Potential & Equilibrium · medium · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans C] (g63)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Cell Potential & Equilibrium',
    $qt63$A Daniell cell operates with the overall reaction \(\text{Zn}(s) + \text{Cu}^{2+}(aq) \rightarrow \text{Zn}^{2+}(aq) + \text{Cu}(s)\), for which the standard cell potential is \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = +1.10\ \text{V}\) and the number of electrons transferred is \(n = 2\). Using the relationship \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = \frac{0.0592}{n}\log K_{eq}\) at \(298\ \text{K}\), which of the following best describes the equilibrium constant \(K_{eq}\) for this reaction and what it implies about the extent of reaction?$qt63$,
    $op63$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(K_{eq} \\approx 10^{19}\\); the reaction proceeds essentially to completion, leaving almost no \\(\\text{Cu}^{2+}\\) at equilibrium"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(K_{eq} \\approx 10^{37}\\); reactants and products are present in comparable amounts at equilibrium"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(K_{eq} \\approx 10^{37}\\); the reaction proceeds essentially to completion, leaving almost no \\(\\text{Cu}^{2+}\\) at equilibrium"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(K_{eq} \\approx 10^{-37}\\); the reaction barely proceeds, leaving almost no \\(\\text{Cu}(s)\\) at equilibrium"}]$op63$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex63$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is C because the equation relating standard cell potential to the equilibrium constant is \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = \frac{0.0592}{n}\log K_{eq}\) at \(298\ \text{K}\). Solving for \(\log K_{eq}\) gives \(\log K_{eq} = \frac{n\,E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}}{0.0592} = \frac{(2)(1.10)}{0.0592} = \frac{2.20}{0.0592} \approx 37.2\), so \(K_{eq} = 10^{37.2} \approx 10^{37}\). Because \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) is positive, the reaction is spontaneous (\(\Delta G^{\circ} = -nFE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} < 0\)) and \(K_{eq}\) is enormous. A \(K_{eq}\) on the order of \(10^{37}\) means products are overwhelmingly favored, so the forward reaction proceeds essentially to completion and the concentration of the limiting reactant \(\text{Cu}^{2+}\) at equilibrium is vanishingly small. (Choice A) This value arises from dividing \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) by \(0.0592\) without multiplying by \(n\): \(\frac{1.10}{0.0592} \approx 18.6\), giving \(\approx 10^{19}\); omitting the factor of \(n = 2\) understates the exponent by about half. (Choice B) This choice computes the correct magnitude of \(K_{eq}\) but misinterprets it; a value as large as \(10^{37}\) is the opposite of "comparable amounts" (which would require \(K_{eq}\) near \(1\)) and instead signals a reaction lying far to the product side. (Choice D) This value reverses the sign of the exponent, as would result from using \(-E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) (treating the spontaneous galvanic reaction as nonspontaneous); a positive \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) requires \(K_{eq} \gg 1\), not \(K_{eq} \ll 1\). Skill 2$ex63$,
    'medium', '4C', $cc63$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc63$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
