-- Q23 [Cell Potential & Equilibrium · medium · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans A] (g65)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Cell Potential & Equilibrium',
    $qt65$At 298 K, the single-electron-transfer reaction \(\text{Ag}^{+}(aq) + \text{Fe}^{2+}(aq) \rightarrow \text{Ag}(s) + \text{Fe}^{3+}(aq)\) has an equilibrium constant \(K_{eq} = 1.0 \times 10^{8}\). Recall that \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -nFE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = -RT\ln K_{eq}\), which at 298 K reduces to \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = \frac{0.0592}{n}\log K_{eq}\ \text{V}\). What is the standard cell potential \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) for this reaction?$qt65$,
    $op65$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(+0.47\\ \\text{V}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(+0.24\\ \\text{V}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(+0.059\\ \\text{V}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(-0.47\\ \\text{V}\\)"}]$op65$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex65$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is A because the cell potential is linked to the equilibrium constant through \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -nFE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = -RT\ln K_{eq}\), which rearranges at 298 K to \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = \frac{0.0592}{n}\log K_{eq}\). The reaction as written transfers exactly one electron from \(\text{Fe}^{2+}\) (oxidized to \(\text{Fe}^{3+}\)) to \(\text{Ag}^{+}\) (reduced to \(\text{Ag}\)), so \(n = 1\). With \(\log K_{eq} = \log(1.0 \times 10^{8}) = 8\): \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = \frac{0.0592}{1}(8) = +0.474 \approx +0.47\ \text{V}\). Because \(K_{eq} > 1\), \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) is positive and the reaction is spontaneous (\(\Delta G^{\circ} < 0\)), consistent with the result. (Choice B) \(+0.24\ \text{V}\) comes from incorrectly dividing by \(n = 2\), \(\frac{0.0592}{2}(8) = 0.237\), as if two electrons were transferred; but the half-reactions \(\text{Ag}^{+} + e^{-} \rightarrow \text{Ag}\) and \(\text{Fe}^{2+} \rightarrow \text{Fe}^{3+} + e^{-}\) each involve only one electron, so \(n = 1\). (Choice C) \(+0.059\ \text{V}\) results from using \(\log K_{eq} = 1\) instead of \(8\), i.e., mistaking the coefficient \(1.0\) in \(1.0 \times 10^{8}\) for the exponent and ignoring the decade power. (Choice D) \(-0.47\ \text{V}\) has the correct magnitude but the wrong sign; a positive \(\log K_{eq}\) (since \(K_{eq} > 1\)) must yield a positive \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\), and the negative sign would wrongly imply a nonspontaneous reaction. Skill 2$ex65$,
    'medium', '4C', $cc65$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc65$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
