-- Q24 [Cell Potential & Equilibrium · medium · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans B] (g66)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Cell Potential & Equilibrium',
    $qt66$A redox reaction transfers three electrons per overall reaction event (\(n = 3\)) and has an equilibrium constant \(K_{eq} = 3.0 \times 10^{-4}\) at 298 K. Using the relationship \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = \frac{0.0592}{n}\log K_{eq}\) (with \(\log 3.0 \approx 0.48\)), what is the standard cell potential for the reaction as written, and is the reaction spontaneous under standard conditions?$qt66$,
    $op66$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(E^{\\circ}_{\\text{cell}} \\approx +0.070\\ \\text{V}\\); spontaneous as written"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(E^{\\circ}_{\\text{cell}} \\approx -0.070\\ \\text{V}\\); nonspontaneous as written"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(E^{\\circ}_{\\text{cell}} \\approx -0.21\\ \\text{V}\\); nonspontaneous as written"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(E^{\\circ}_{\\text{cell}} \\approx -0.079\\ \\text{V}\\); nonspontaneous as written"}]$op66$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex66$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is B because the relationship \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = \frac{0.0592}{n}\log K_{eq}\) (equivalently \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -nFE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = -RT\ln K_{eq}\)) ties the sign of \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) directly to the magnitude of \(K_{eq}\). Because \(K_{eq} = 3.0 \times 10^{-4} < 1\), its logarithm is negative: \(\log(3.0 \times 10^{-4}) = \log 3.0 + \log(10^{-4}) \approx 0.48 - 4 = -3.52\). Substituting, \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = \frac{0.0592}{3}(-3.52) = (0.0197)(-3.52) \approx -0.070\ \text{V}\). A negative \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) corresponds to \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -nFE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} > 0\) (here \(\Delta G^{\circ} \approx +20\ \text{kJ/mol}\)), so the reaction is nonspontaneous as written under standard conditions — consistent with \(K_{eq} < 1\) favoring reactants. (Choice A) reverses the sign by treating \(\log K_{eq}\) as positive (or by assuming \(K_{eq} > 1\)); a value below 1 must give a negative logarithm and therefore a negative \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\), making the reaction nonspontaneous, not spontaneous. (Choice C) omits dividing by \(n\), computing \(0.0592 \times (-3.52) \approx -0.21\ \text{V}\); the three-electron transfer requires the factor \(\frac{0.0592}{n} = \frac{0.0592}{3}\), which shrinks the magnitude roughly threefold. (Choice D) approximates \(\log(3.0 \times 10^{-4})\) as simply \(-4\) (dropping the \(+0.48\) contribution of the leading 3.0), giving \(\frac{0.0592}{3}(-4) \approx -0.079\ \text{V}\) instead of the correct \(-0.070\ \text{V}\). Skill 2$ex66$,
    'medium', '4C', $cc66$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc66$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
