-- Q25 [Cell Potential & Equilibrium · hard · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans C] (g67)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Cell Potential & Equilibrium',
    $qt67$An acidic galvanic cell is constructed at 298 K in which dichromate is reduced to chromium(III) at the cathode while iodide is oxidized to iodine at the anode, according to the overall reaction \(\text{Cr}_2\text{O}_7^{2-} + 14\,\text{H}^+ + 6\,\text{I}^- \rightarrow 2\,\text{Cr}^{3+} + 3\,\text{I}_2 + 7\,\text{H}_2\text{O}\). The relevant standard reduction potentials are \(\text{Cr}_2\text{O}_7^{2-} + 14\,\text{H}^+ + 6\,e^- \rightarrow 2\,\text{Cr}^{3+} + 7\,\text{H}_2\text{O}\), \(E^{\circ} = +1.33\ \text{V}\), and \(\text{I}_2 + 2\,e^- \rightarrow 2\,\text{I}^-\), \(E^{\circ} = +0.54\ \text{V}\). Using \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = \frac{0.0592}{n}\log K_{eq}\) at 298 K, which value is closest to the equilibrium constant \(K_{eq}\) for the overall reaction as written?$qt67$,
    $op67$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(K_{eq} \\approx 1 \\times 10^{-80}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(K_{eq} \\approx 5 \\times 10^{26}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(K_{eq} \\approx 1 \\times 10^{80}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(K_{eq} \\approx 3 \\times 10^{189}\\)"}]$op67$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex67$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is C because the problem requires three linked steps: (1) combine the two standard half-cell potentials into \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\), (2) determine \(n\) from the balanced electron transfer, and (3) antilog the relationship between \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) and \(K_{eq}\). The dichromate couple is the cathode (reduction), so \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = E^{\circ}_{\text{red,cathode}} - E^{\circ}_{\text{red,anode}} = (+1.33\ \text{V}) - (+0.54\ \text{V}) = +0.79\ \text{V}\); the anode potential is subtracted directly and is not separately sign-flipped. For \(n\), the cathode half-reaction transfers 6 electrons while the iodine couple transfers only 2, so the iodine half is multiplied by 3 to match, giving 6 electrons exchanged overall; therefore \(n = 6\). Note that \(E^{\circ}\) is intensive and is not multiplied by any coefficient. Solving \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = \frac{0.0592}{n}\log K_{eq}\) for \(\log K_{eq}\) gives \(\log K_{eq} = \frac{n\,E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}}{0.0592} = \frac{6 \times 0.79}{0.0592} = \frac{4.74}{0.0592} \approx 80\), so \(K_{eq} \approx 10^{80}\), which is closest to \(1 \times 10^{80}\); the large positive value is consistent with the spontaneous (\(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} > 0\), \(\Delta G^{\circ} < 0\)) galvanic reaction. (Choice A) \(1 \times 10^{-80}\) arises from reversing the cell-potential subtraction to \(0.54 - 1.33 = -0.79\ \text{V}\), which yields \(\log K_{eq} \approx -80\); this would describe the nonspontaneous reverse reaction, not the reaction as written. (Choice B) \(5 \times 10^{26}\) results from using \(n = 2\) (the electrons in the iodine half-reaction as written) instead of scaling both halves to the 6 electrons actually exchanged, giving \(\log K_{eq} = \frac{2 \times 0.79}{0.0592} \approx 27\). (Choice D) \(3 \times 10^{189}\) comes from adding the two reduction potentials, \(1.33 + 0.54 = 1.87\ \text{V}\), rather than subtracting them, inflating \(\log K_{eq}\) to about \(190\). Skill 2$ex67$,
    'hard', '4C', $cc67$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc67$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
