-- Q26 [Cell Potential & Equilibrium · hard · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans D] (g68)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Cell Potential & Equilibrium',
    $qt68$Two distinct galvanic cells are each studied at 298 K. Cell I has a standard cell potential \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = +0.50\ \text{V}\) and transfers \(n = 1\) mole of electrons per mole of reaction as written. Cell II has \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = +0.30\ \text{V}\) and transfers \(n = 3\) moles of electrons per mole of reaction as written. Using \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -nFE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = -RT\ln K_{eq}\), which statement correctly compares the two cells?$qt68$,
    $op68$[{"label":"A","text":"Cell I has both the larger \\(K_{eq}\\) and the more negative \\(\\Delta G^{\\circ}\\), because its larger \\(E^{\\circ}_{\\text{cell}}\\) governs both quantities."},{"label":"B","text":"The two cells have equal \\(K_{eq}\\) and equal \\(\\Delta G^{\\circ}\\), because \\(E^{\\circ}_{\\text{cell}}\\) is an intensive property and is therefore independent of \\(n\\)."},{"label":"C","text":"Cell I has the larger \\(K_{eq}\\) but Cell II has the more negative \\(\\Delta G^{\\circ}\\), because \\(K_{eq}\\) tracks \\(E^{\\circ}_{\\text{cell}}\\) while \\(\\Delta G^{\\circ}\\) tracks \\(nE^{\\circ}_{\\text{cell}}\\)."},{"label":"D","text":"Cell II has both the larger \\(K_{eq}\\) and the more negative \\(\\Delta G^{\\circ}\\), because the product \\(nE^{\\circ}_{\\text{cell}}\\) is larger for Cell II."}]$op68$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex68$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is D because both thermodynamic descriptors of the equilibrium position depend on the PRODUCT \(nE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\), not on \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) alone. Start from \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -nFE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\). For Cell I, \(nE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = (1)(0.50) = 0.50\ \text{V}\), giving \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -(1)(96{,}485)(0.50) \approx -48.2\ \text{kJ/mol}\). For Cell II, \(nE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = (3)(0.30) = 0.90\ \text{V}\), giving \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -(3)(96{,}485)(0.30) \approx -86.8\ \text{kJ/mol}\). Because \(0.90\ \text{V} > 0.50\ \text{V}\), Cell II has the more negative \(\Delta G^{\circ}\). The same product controls \(K_{eq}\): combining \(-nFE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = -RT\ln K_{eq}\) gives \(\log K_{eq} = \frac{nE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}}{0.0592}\) at 298 K. For Cell I, \(\log K_{eq} = 0.50/0.0592 \approx 8.4\) (\(K_{eq}\approx 10^{8}\)); for Cell II, \(\log K_{eq} = 0.90/0.0592 \approx 15.2\) (\(K_{eq}\approx 10^{15}\)). Thus the cell with the SMALLER \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) (Cell II) nonetheless has both the larger \(K_{eq}\) and the more negative \(\Delta G^{\circ}\), because its larger \(n\) more than compensates. (Choice A) This treats \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) alone as the controlling quantity and ignores the factor of \(n\); since \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) is intensive it does not by itself set \(K_{eq}\) or \(\Delta G^{\circ}\), so concluding Cell I wins on both counts reverses the actual ranking. (Choice B) This misapplies the intensive nature of \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\): although \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) does not scale with \(n\), the extensive quantities \(\Delta G^{\circ}\) and \(\ln K_{eq}\) both contain an explicit factor of \(n\), so the cells are not equal. (Choice C) This splits the two descriptors, correctly tying \(\Delta G^{\circ}\) to \(nE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) but incorrectly tying \(K_{eq}\) to \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) alone; in fact \(\log K_{eq} = nE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}/0.0592\), so \(K_{eq}\) depends on the same product, and both quantities favor Cell II together. Skill 2$ex68$,
    'hard', '4C', $cc68$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc68$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
